# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
phaedo-opposites.md:17: argue/readings: S12 is read 2 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r3 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.2 have nothing from it; phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.3 has "∀x (has_opposite(x) → generated_from_opposite(x))"; they differ when only "life is generated out of its opposite", "life has an opposite", "living is generated out of its opposite", "living has an opposite" and "dead has an opposite" are true, which the first allows
phaedo-opposites.md:17: argue/answered: S12: answered no, which keeps phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "life is generated out of its opposite", "life has an opposite", "living is generated out of its opposite", "living has an opposite" and "dead has an opposite" are true and "dead is generated out of its opposite" is false. Is S12, "And is this true of all opposites? and are we convinced that all of them are generated out of opposites?", true then?
phaedo-opposites.md:25: argue/readings: S17 is read 2 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r3 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.2 have "∀x (has_opposite(x) → generated_from_opposite(x))"; phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "life is generated out of its opposite", "life has an opposite", "living is generated out of its opposite", "living has an opposite" and "dead has an opposite" are true, which the second allows
phaedo-opposites.md:25: argue/answered: S17: answered no, which keeps phaedo-opposites.logic.r3 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.2, to: Suppose "life is generated out of its opposite", "life has an opposite", "living is generated out of its opposite", "living has an opposite" and "dead has an opposite" are true and "dead is generated out of its opposite" is false. Is S17, "And this necessarily holds of all opposites, even though not always expressed in words--they are really generated out of one another, and there is a passing or process from one to the other of them?", true then?
phaedo-opposites.md:29: argue/readings: S19 is read 2 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r3 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.2 have nothing from it; phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.3 has "has_opposite(living)"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
phaedo-opposites.md:29: argue/answered: S19: answered no, which keeps phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "living has an opposite" is false. Is S19, "Well, and is there not an opposite of life, as sleep is the opposite of waking?", true then?
phaedo-opposites.md:45: argue/readings: S31 is read 3 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r3 has "has_opposite(life)"; phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.2 has "has_opposite(living) ∧ has_opposite(dead)"; they differ when only "life has an opposite" and "living has an opposite" are true, which the first allows; phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.3 has "has_opposite(dead)"
phaedo-opposites.md:45: argue/answered: S31: answered yes, which keeps phaedo-opposites.logic.r3, to: Suppose "life has an opposite" and "living has an opposite" are true and "dead has an opposite" is false. Is S31, "Is not death opposed to life?", true then?
phaedo-opposites.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r3 has "generated_from_opposite(life) → souls_exist_below"; phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.2 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.3 have "generated_from_opposite(living) → souls_exist_below"; they differ when only "living is generated out of its opposite" is true, which the first allows
phaedo-opposites.md:25: argue/quantifier: phaedo-opposites.logic.r3 P1 (S17): the sentence says "not always", but P1 says it of every one: it reads "for every x, if x has an opposite, then x is generated out of its opposite"
phaedo-opposites.md:25: argue/quantifier: phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.2 P1 (S17): the sentence says "not always", but P1 says it of every one: it reads "for every x, if x has an opposite, then x is generated out of its opposite"
phaedo-opposites.md:29: argue/negation: phaedo-opposites.logic.r3.3 P2 (S19): the sentence says "not", but P2 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "living has an opposite"
